# bridges/did/ — W3C DID 1.1 bridge (atlas §3.6, bridge seam)

Governing spec: genesis/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-17-did-bridge-identity-resolution-design.md.

## The conformance discipline (authoritative; the inject rule above is the reminder, the tests are the gate)

1. **Schema**: schemas/did-document-1.1.schema.json — hand-derived from the W3C DID 1.1 TR.
   It validates the structure of known properties and NEVER forbids extensions
   (additionalProperties stays true; DID documents legally carry extension properties).
2. **Suite**: did-tests/tests/did11_conformance.rs validates every document this crate emits,
   plus the fidelity fixtures, against the schema — and asserts the v1.1 context pin.
3. **Honesty about the mechanism**: the rule names **no validator**. The conformance check is the
   suite (`cargo test -p did-tests --test did11_conformance`) and the schema — the rule's job is to
   surface them at the moment a wire shape is edited, at `inject` class (teach, never block). A
   `validator:` ref naming a detector the resolver does not implement advertises enforcement the
   rule does not serve; the phantom `epr:validator-did11-conformance` ref was dropped 2026-08-02
   (plan task P4.1) with the rule's teaching intact. If a real conformance validator-EPR is ever
   registered, the rule graduates then — the `why:` already carries the destination.
4. **History**: the three drifts this gate exists for all occurred at birth (2026-07-17):
   @context nearly pinned to 1.0; extension properties dropped on round-trip; did:web %40
   host-confusion SSRF. Review caught them once; the suite catches them forever.
